== 13. Of the Parent

So far we have treated the first couple of which the family consists, namely married folks. Now the others follow, and they are of two sorts: either those by whom the family is increased and multiplied, or those who are helps to it.

Those by whom the family is enlarged are also of two sorts: parents and children.

Parents are those who have power and authority over children.

The duties of parents are chiefly two: one, to bring up their children; the other, to bestow them when they have brought them up.

As to the education or bringing up of children, the parents’ care must be both that they may live and also that they may live well, Eph 6:4.

As to the preserving of the child’s life, many duties are required of the parents, chiefly four. First, the mother is herself to nurse the infant and to wrap it in swaddling cloths. Paul commends it as a mark of a good wife that she nurses her own children, 1 Tim 5:10. So Sarah nursed Isaac, Gen 21:7. Hannah nursed Samuel, 1 Sam 1:22. David in all likelihood was nursed by his mother, Ps 22:9: “You made me secure at my mother’s breast.” And our Savior Christ was also nursed by Mary his mother, Luke 11:27, and swaddled, Luke 2:7. Now the reason for this is plain. God has given milk to women who bear children, and has so appointed that no food is more natural to the child than the mother’s milk. And nature itself teaches this in the course of other creatures. The earth nourishes what it brings forth. The fruit the tree bears is fed by the sap that comes from its root. Yes, among the brute beasts, even those that are most savage, such as tigers, the mothers nurse the young they bring forth.

Yet in case of want of health or ability, or any other just hindrance, the mother may in fairness be excused, and the child committed to a nurse. And so we read that Rebekah, the daughter of Bethuel and afterward the wife of Isaac, had a nurse, Gen 24:59, whose name was Deborah, Gen 35:8. And Pharaoh’s daughter, in a case of necessity, committed Moses to be nursed by a Hebrew woman, whom she took to be not his mother but a stranger; and her deed was approved by God, Exod 2:7, 9.

The second duty of the parent is to provide food, drink and clothing for his child. Our Savior Christ says that parents, even by the light of nature, though they are evil, will not deny or withhold necessary food from their children. Matt 7:9, 10: “Which of you#super[+], if his son asks for bread, will give him a stone? Or if he asks for a fish, will give him a snake?” And Paul says, “If anyone does not provide for his own, and especially his own household, he has denied the faith and is worse than an unbeliever” (1 Tim 5:8).

The third duty is, when God gives ability and means, to lay up something for the future maintenance of the child. 2 Cor 12:14: “… For children should not have to save up for their parents, but parents for their children.” Prov 19:14: “Houses and wealth are inherited from fathers.”

The fourth is to observe both the inclination and the natural gifts of body and mind that are in the child, and accordingly to place it in some honorable calling and course of life. Prov 20:11: “Even a child is known by his actions—whether his conduct is pure and upright.” In the church history#footnote[Ruffin. Eccles. hist. lib. 1. c. 14.] written by Rufinus it is recorded that Alexander, bishop of Alexandria, saw from afar, in a place near the sea, a company of children playing together, and in their play imitating him in various ministerial actions. When he had watched them for a while, he perceived that they examined and baptized one another according to the solemn order used in the congregation. He made some of his clergy acquainted with this, and gave order that the children should be sent for and brought before him. Then he asked them what game they played, and in what manner they played with one another. At first the children, out of fear, denied what they had done; yet on further examination they confessed that they had made Athanasius (who afterward became bishop of that place) their bishop, and that he had instructed and baptized several of their company. Alexander further asked both Athanasius himself and those who were said to have been baptized what questions Athanasius had asked them, and what answers they had given. By their speeches he perceived that they had framed themselves, though in play, to the church’s proceedings in those actions. So, after conference with his clergy, he determined that those who had first been asked, and had answered fully according to the form used in that church, and had thereupon been baptized, should not be rebaptized. And as for Athanasius and the other children who had done the part of ministers in their kind, he sent for their parents and gave them a strict charge that they should be brought up in learning and religion, so that in time they might be fit for the public ministry. We read that the heathen took a like course to judge the inclination and aptness of their children.#footnote[Nazianz. epist. ad Eudoxam.] The Athenians made a law that before children were placed in any calling, they should first be brought into a public place where instruments belonging to all sorts of trades were laid out. And whatever kind of instrument any of them took most delight in, the child was thought to be most fit for the trade to which that instrument belonged, and was afterward set to it with good success.

Now the parents’ first and chief care must be for the church: that those of their children who have the readiest wit and are endowed with the best gifts be consecrated to God, and brought up in the study of the Scriptures, to serve afterward in the ministry of the church. So Hannah dedicated her son Samuel to God by vow, 1 Sam 1:11: “And she made a vow, pleading, ‘O #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts, if only You will … give Your maidservant a son, then I will dedicate him to the #smallcaps[Lord] all the days of his life.’” And she did according to her vow, and the Lord blessed her son Samuel, who in time became a great prophet.

The next point of the education of children is to provide that they may live well and lead a godly life. To this end parents must do three things.

First, they are to take care that the child, as soon as may be after it is born, is admitted into the true church of God by baptism, and has a fit name given to it.

#emph[Secondly, they are to endeavor to sow the seeds of godliness and religion in the heart of the child as soon as it comes to the use of reason and understanding; and as it grows in years, care must be taken that it grows in knowledge and grace. To this end serves the vow and promise made in baptism, which parents are bound to call on the child to remember when it comes of age. It was the godly care of Timothy’s parents,] 2 Tim 3:14: “But as for you, continue in the things you have learned and firmly believed, since you know from whom you have learned them.” v. 15: “From infancy you have known the Holy Scriptures, which are able to make you wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus.” #emph[This was God’s commandment to the parents of his people,] Deut 6:7: “And you shall teach them diligently to your children and speak of them when you sit at home.” #emph[And] v. 20: “In the future, when your son asks, ‘What is the meaning of the decrees and statutes and ordinances that the #smallcaps[Lord] our God has commanded you#super[+]?’ then you are to tell him, ‘We were slaves of Pharaoh in Egypt …’” #emph[This was observed by the Israelites in the ages that followed:] Ps 44:1: “We have heard with our ears, O God; our fathers have told us the work You did in their days, in the days of old.”

Thirdly, the first instruction of children in learning and religion must be so ordered that they may take it with delight. For this purpose they may at times be allowed to play in a moderate manner, and to refresh themselves in recreations fitting for their years. And if they do amiss in either, they are to be restrained by the bridle of discipline: first by reproof in words, and when that will not help, by the rod of correction. Zech 8:5: “And the streets of the city will be filled with boys and girls playing there.” Prov 29:15: “A rod of correction imparts wisdom, but a child left to himself disgraces his mother.” v. 17: “Discipline your son, and he will give you rest; he will bring delight to your soul.” Heb 12:9: “We have all had earthly fathers who disciplined us, and we respected them.”

Yet in this point two extremes are carefully to be avoided: that the parent be neither too severe nor too indulgent toward the child. Against severity Paul gives his rule, Eph 6:4: “Fathers, do not provoke your#super[+] children to wrath,” that is, by too much harshness in government. For leniency we have old Eli’s example, 1 Sam 2:23, 24: “‘Why are you#super[+] doing these things?’ Eli said to his sons. ‘I hear about your#super[+] wicked deeds from all these people. No, my sons …’” So much for education.

#emph[The second general duty of parents is the bestowing of their children. This bestowing is when parents, knowing their children to be fit for marriage and not to have the gift of continency, use their best endeavor to provide matches for them in time, or at least advise them to it, by themselves or by their friends:] 1 Cor 7:36: “If anyone thinks he is acting improperly toward his virgin daughter, if she is past the flower of her age and need so requires, … they should get married.” v. 38: “He who gives her in marriage does well.” Jer 29:6: “Take wives … Take wives for your#super[+] sons and give your daughters in marriage,” and so on. Gen 28:1: “So Isaac called for Jacob and blessed him. ‘Do not take a wife from the Canaanite women,’ he commanded. ‘Go at once to Paddan-aram … and take a wife from among the daughters of Laban, your mother’s brother.’” Judg 14:2, of Samson: “So he returned and told his father and mother, ‘I have seen a daughter of the Philistines in Timnah. Now get her for me as a wife.’”

This duty is nowadays carelessly neglected by parents, and its fruit is dangerous. For from this it comes about that their children often either commit whoredom or enter into wicked and ungodly marriages.

In the choice of a husband or wife, the parents ought to have greater regard for piety and wisdom than for beauty and riches, or any other outward blessings. Yet where they all meet together, the party so qualified is to be received all the more thankfully. On these grounds the priest or prince of Midian gave his daughter Zipporah to Moses, and Moses agreed to take her, Exod 2:19: “An Egyptian rescued us from the shepherds. … He even drew water for us and watered the flock. … Moses agreed to stay with the man, and he gave his daughter Zipporah to Moses in marriage.” Josh 15:16: “And Caleb said, ‘To the man who strikes down Kiriath-sepher and captures it, I will give my daughter Acsah in marriage.’” Indeed, it is a sin to marry only for beauty, without consideration of the better things. So the old world sinned, when “the sons of God saw that the daughters of men were beautiful, and they took as wives whomever they chose” (Gen 6:2). And so did Samson, who, only on the sight of the woman in Timnah, of the daughters of the Philistines, without further inquiry, desired that she might be given him as his wife, Judg 14:2.

Again, it is fitting that parents should deal moderately with their children in this case, and by no means undertake to force and compel them to marry this or that party. This was the just commendation of Rebekah’s parents: though they were idolaters, yet they had such regard for fairness that they first called her and asked her consent before they sent her away with Abraham’s servant to be married to Isaac, Gen 24:58. Now if the parents fail in their duty in this respect, the son or daughter may lawfully lay the matter first before their kinsfolk, and afterward (if need be) before the minister or magistrate, and seek redress by their means and direction.
